Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.240 --> 00:00:01.750 align:start position:0%
 
we're taking a closer look at  developments in the south pacific today  specifically the solomon islands it is  planning a security deal with china and  the idea of chinese warships based less  than 2 000 kilometers off the australian  coast off its northeast has set alarm  bells ringing in fact even the united  states is worried the white house has  plans to send its top asia official kurt  campbell to the small nation later this  month  the ongoing battle for influence between  china and the west is shifting to the  south pacific  beijing is upping its presence a draft  agreement would allow chinese police and  soldiers to patrol the solomon islands  the five eyes intelligence alliance  britain canada australia and new zealand  fears china is eyeing a naval base there  the solomons have long been seen as  australia's backyard  the archipelago is a long way from china  but it's just a three-hour flight from  brisbane  australian and new zealand leaders are  not happy  the what the reports that we've seen are  not a surprise to us and a reminder of  the constant pressure and threats  that present in our region to our own  national security look we new zealand  has long expressed an opinion that we do  not want to see the militarization of  the pacific that we believe we can  support one another with defense and  security needs as they arise and new  zealand has a history of doing that in  the solomon islands  solomon islands switched diplomatic ties  from taiwan to beijing in 2019 which  partly fueled discontent that led to  riots in the capital honiara in november  last year  chinese businesses were attacked in the  unrest  solomon islands prime minister manesse  sagavar dismisses critics of the treaty  with china as lunatics disrespectful of  the island's sovereignty  we have no intention mr speaker of  pitching into any geopolitical power  struggle  to suggest that mr speaker is simply  ludicrous  as a sovereign country mr speaker  we will continue  to collaborate  to ensure  that what solomon islands needs  in its security space are addressed  collectively  with our development partners  china wants to build a blue water navy  capable of projecting power around the  world  to make this work it needs bases from  which to service a strike force  china tries to play down fears it wants  to expand in the region and blames  australia for promoting u.s led  competition  as two independent and sovereign  countries china and solomon islands can  carry out normal law enforcement and  security cooperation  on the basis of equal treatment and  mutual benefit  the west has been stunned by the speed  with which china has moved to militarize  the man-made islands in the south china  sea  a move to solomon islands increases the  risk of escalation and potential  conflict  joining us in the studio is dw's  clifford conan clifford tell us more  about this proposed security deal i  thought australia had some kind of  relationship with the solomon islands it  does it already has a security deal and  it also has a very complicated and  intertwined relationship with the  solomon islands um you know as as we  mentioned the report it's a two-hour  flight from brisbane um and the south  pacific is seen very much as australia's  backyard its sphere of influence  and it gives 1.3 billion us dollars  every year in development aid and it has  various other security guarantees when  there was unrest there in november last  year they sent um security forces there  to to shore up the unrest so they do  have this very complicated relationship  but um it's also very uh um difficult  balancing act in many ways for australia  as we can see in a quote here from from  scott morrison  as a friend of solomon islands we remain  their first call for security we remain  the first call when it comes to the  issues uh that concern them right across  their development eye the suggestion  that somehow some seem to be making that  that solomon islands is somehow under  the control of australia i think is  offensive to the solomon islands they  are a sovereign nation i respect their  independence and they will make their  own decisions about their own  sovereignty what we have been doing is  ensuring that they are fully aware of  the risks and the security matters that  are not only of concern to australia but  ireland's pacific nations across the  pacific  so i think there what that comment shows  that if you look at it from the solomon  islands perspective you know they're  151st in the 189 uh countries on the u.n  human development index so it's a poor  country  it needs australia's help but then china  can also offer economic guarantees as  well as security guarantees  so it's a very vulnerable country with a  lot of needs  i think a lot of people in asia probably  looking at the situation and wondering  the parallel with the south china seas  because this potential security  agreement can quickly turn into a very  permanent military installation can you  talk a little bit about that  sure well china wants to establish  itself as the premier power broker in  the indo-pacific and it's done this  through various means through trying to  um broker deals with various countries  usually economic guarantees uh in some  ways solomon islands is unusual because  it's it's a straightforward security  guarantee which is something that we've  seen in djibouti in africa before but um  we haven't really seen it so much in the  region near to china so um it does have  a lot of parallels there um we've seen  how china has militarized these three  islands  which are near the philippines which has  led recently to the philippines talking  to japan all these countries all of  china's neighbors basically have some  kind of territorial dispute with china  and because of china's efforts to expand  its influence so um there are parallels  with that and um  i think we're going to see more and more  of that as things go on now australia is  part of the quad together with the  united states uh india japan and you  also recently have had the agreement uh  between  australia the uk and the u.s in terms of  providing nuclear-powered submarines so  china can sort of say hey this is  encirclement it feels like it and we  have the right to engage with the  solomon islands if we want to well china  generally says whenever this comes up it  says that it's only doing what the u.s  has done before that its sphere of  influence is pretty much where it  decides um the solomon islands is a long  way away from china  now you can see it as an extension  because it's in the pacific area and  china wants to basically control the  pacific um but so encirclement is one  argument but in reality this is more  just about trying to control the pacific  um and it's interesting mark milley the  joint chairman of the joint chiefs of  staff he said recently that china is  trying to um  is trying to break the established  system of rule of law and one of the  areas that he focused particularly on  was the navy and i think um watching  watching the sort of china's efforts to  control the marine they're developing a  lot of their technological advances are  focused on on naval vessels for example  they're going to have a new aircraft  carrier which launches by catapult and  they're also trying to develop an  aircraft carrier that's a  nuclear-powered aircraft carrier so  there's a lot of technological  advancement there's a lot of defense  spending going into controlling the navy  and that i think you know shows just how  focused  the the chinese china is on the on the  so china sea and the whole pacific yeah  i mean if you look at the maps the  solomon islands are so far away from  china so really quickly i mean  ratcheting up of tensions in the pacific  huh i think so i think that's what we're  looking at now for now there's just  going to be a succession of these sort  of deals being struck there's a lot of  small countries in the area a lot of  small island states a lot of them with  very great strategic importance so  certainly more tensions in the asia  pacific  clifford coonan thank you so much as  always for joining us  you

